      <string id="Console_ConnReach">Reach – directly or through the RemoteHub Broker</string>
      <string id="Console_ConnReach_Help">Fixes this setting for every connection on this PC. It wins over the global defaults, the folders and the connection, which keep their own value but don't use it; the connection editor shows it greyed out at every level. A settings file shared with PCs without this policy keeps working there as before.

Applies to devices in the synced folder of a RemoteHub Broker – the agent and RDP alike.

Enabled: the value chosen here. Automatic tries the device's own address for 1.5 seconds, then goes through the broker; Directly only never uses the broker; Through the RemoteHub Broker only never tries the device's own address.

Disabled or Not configured: the user decides (default: Automatic).

With "Allow the RemoteHub Broker" disabled, devices are reached only directly.

Registry: Reach (REG_SZ: Automatic, Direct, Broker).</string>
      <string id="Console_ConnReach_Automatic">Automatic – directly, otherwise through the RemoteHub Broker</string>
      <string id="Console_ConnReach_Direct">Directly only</string>
      <string id="Console_ConnReach_Broker">Through the RemoteHub Broker only</string>

      <string id="Console_ConnAgentAutoSignIn">Sign in automatically when nobody is signed in</string>
      <string id="Console_ConnAgentAutoSignIn_Help">Fixes this setting for every connection on this PC. It wins over the global defaults, the folders and the connection, which keep their own value but don't use it; the connection editor shows it greyed out at every level. A settings file shared with PCs without this policy keeps working there as before.

Enabled: RemoteHub signs in automatically through the agent where the device allows it.

Disabled: it doesn't.

Not configured: the user decides (default: no).

"Turn off automatic sign-in through the agent" wins over it.

Registry: AgentAutoSignIn (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>

      <string id="Console_ConnAgentAutoSignInEndAction">When an automatic sign-in's connection ends</string>
      <string id="Console_ConnAgentAutoSignInEndAction_Help">Fixes this setting for every connection on this PC. It wins over the global defaults, the folders and the connection, which keep their own value but don't use it; the connection editor shows it greyed out at every level. A settings file shared with PCs without this policy keeps working there as before.

Enabled: the value chosen here.

Disabled or Not configured: the user decides (default: Lock the session).

The device's AutoSignInEndAction policy may decide instead.

Registry: AgentAutoSignInEndAction (REG_SZ: Lock, SignOut).</string>
      <string id="Console_ConnAgentAutoSignInEndAction_Lock">Lock the session</string>
      <string id="Console_ConnAgentAutoSignInEndAction_SignOut">Sign out</string>

      <!-- Console: phase 5 -->
      <string id="Console_AllowConnectionEditing">Allow changing folders, connections and shares</string>
      <string id="Console_AllowConnectionEditing_Help">Enabled or Not configured: allowed.

Disabled: The tree is read-only: creating, editing, duplicating, moving and deleting folders, connections and shares isn't offered (menus, the New button, F2, Del, Ctrl+N, drag and drop), and importing .rdp files or a backup is refused. Connecting, the tools and the settings window still work. For connections IT provides through a shared settings folder.

This binds RemoteHub on this PC only.

Registry: AllowConnectionEditing (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>

      <string id="Console_AllowSsh">Allow SSH connections</string>
      <string id="Console_AllowSsh_Help">Enabled or Not configured: allowed.

Disabled: SSH connections (a terminal tab) can't be opened or added; they stay in the tree. SFTP shares are set by "Allow SFTP shares".

This binds RemoteHub on this PC only.

Registry: AllowSsh (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>

      <string id="Console_AllowSavedPasswords">Allow saving passwords</string>
      <string id="Console_AllowSavedPasswords_Help">Enabled or Not configured: allowed.

Disabled: No password can be stored in connections, folders, shares, the global defaults or saved credentials (the password boxes are greyed out), and stored passwords aren't used: RemoteHub (or Windows, for RDP) asks when connecting and keeps what is typed in memory only. On this PC's own settings the stored passwords are removed at the next start, after a notice; a shared settings file keeps them, because other PCs may allow them.

This binds RemoteHub on this PC only.

Registry: AllowSavedPasswords (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>

      <string id="Console_AllowPasswordCopy">Allow copying a stored password to the clipboard</string>
      <string id="Console_AllowPasswordCopy_Help">Enabled or Not configured: allowed.

Disabled: When Windows asks for the password during an RDP sign-in, the stored password is not copied to the clipboard; the connection setting "When Windows asks for the password" is greyed out.

This binds RemoteHub on this PC only.

Registry: AllowPasswordCopy (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>

      <string id="Console_StartupProtection">Startup protection</string>
      <string id="Console_StartupProtection_Help">Fixes this setting of RemoteHub on this PC.

Required: at start without startup protection, RemoteHub asks for a startup password and closes if none is set; it can't be turned off. Off: it can't be turned on (for kiosk or jump-server PCs); where it is already on, it can still be turned off. Read at start.

Not configured: the user decides.

Registry: StartupProtection (REG_SZ: Required, Off).</string>
      <string id="Console_StartupProtection_Required">Required – RemoteHub opens nothing until it is set up</string>
      <string id="Console_StartupProtection_Off">Off – it can't be turned on</string>

      <string id="Console_AllowWindowsHello">Allow Windows Hello for startup protection</string>
      <string id="Console_AllowWindowsHello_Help">Enabled or Not configured: allowed.

Disabled: Windows Hello can't be set up for startup protection, and where it is set up it isn't offered when unlocking – the startup password is asked.

This binds RemoteHub on this PC only.

Registry: AllowWindowsHello (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>

      <string id="Console_AutoLockMinutes">Lock RemoteHub after</string>
      <string id="Console_AutoLockMinutes_Help">Enabled: the value set here (minutes without mouse or keyboard input on this PC).

Disabled or Not configured: the user decides in Startup protection (default: never).

Needs startup protection: without it there is nothing to lock, and the value is ignored. The choice in Startup protection is greyed out.

Registry: AutoLockMinutes (REG_DWORD), 1–1440.</string>

      <string id="Console_SettingsLocation">Where settings are stored</string>
      <string id="Console_SettingsLocation_Help">Fixes this setting of RemoteHub on this PC.

This PC only: a shared folder the user chose isn't used. The shared settings folder: users can't switch back to this PC only; set the folder with "Shared settings folder", which wins over This PC only when both are set. Read at start.

Not configured: the user decides.

Registry: SettingsLocation (REG_SZ: Local, Shared).</string>
      <string id="Console_SettingsLocation_Local">This PC only</string>
      <string id="Console_SettingsLocation_Shared">The shared settings folder</string>

      <string id="Console_DiagnosticLog">Write the diagnostic log</string>
      <string id="Console_DiagnosticLog_Help">Enabled or Not configured: RemoteHub writes its diagnostic log (%APPDATA%\RemoteHub\logs\remotehub.log): connection and password-prompt events, never passwords, user names or host names.

Disabled: no diagnostic log is written.

The Logs page in Settings shows what is in effect.

Registry: DiagnosticLog (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>

      <string id="Console_DiagnosticLogRetentionDays">Keep the diagnostic log</string>
      <string id="Console_DiagnosticLogRetentionDays_Help">Enabled: the value set here (days).

Disabled or Not configured: 30 days.

Older entries are removed when RemoteHub starts.

Registry: DiagnosticLogRetentionDays (REG_DWORD), 1–3650.</string>

      <!-- Agent: features -->
      <string id="Agent_PowerShellEnabled">Allow PowerShell, files, registry, services and processes</string>
      <string id="Agent_PowerShellEnabled_Help">Enabled or Not configured: the agent offers PowerShell sessions to the accounts in "Who may use PowerShell, files, registry, services and processes". RemoteHub's PowerShell, file transfer, registry, services and processes tabs all run in such a session.

Disabled: nobody gets a PowerShell session through the agent, so none of these tools work through it, and RemoteHub can't update the agent through itself. The remote desktop and jump connections aren't affected. RemoteHub says the tools are turned off on the device.

The agent can't turn the tools off one by one: they all run in the same PowerShell session. RemoteHub's own policies can hide single tools on the PCs it runs on.

Registry: PowerShellEnabled (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>

      <!-- Agent: rights -->
      <string id="Agent_AllowedGroups">Default groups for every right</string>
      <string id="Agent_AllowedGroups_Help">The groups that get every right not set on its own below: PowerShell, the screen, the sign-in screen, the privacy screen and the jump host. Replaces the "Allowed accounts" chosen when the agent was installed.

"Who may only see the screen", "Who may connect without asking the signed-in user" and "Who may sign in automatically" are never given by this: they stay "nobody" unless set on their own.

Enter one group per line, as a SID (recommended, for example S-1-5-32-544 for Administrators) or a name (CONTOSO\Helpdesk).

Not configured: the groups chosen when the agent was installed (Administrators by default).

Registry: AllowedGroups (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_PowerShell">Who may use PowerShell, files, registry, services and processes</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_PowerShell_Help">Groups that may open a PowerShell session through the agent. RemoteHub's PowerShell, file transfer, registry, services and processes tabs all use it.

Enter one group per line, as a SID (recommended, for example S-1-5-32-544 for Administrators) or a name (CONTOSO\Helpdesk). Names differ between Windows languages; SIDs don't. To allow nobody, enter S-1-0-0.

Not configured: the groups of "Default groups for every right", else the groups chosen when the agent was installed (Administrators by default).

Registry: Rights\PowerShell (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_Desktop">Who may see and control the screen</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_Desktop_Help">Groups that may connect to the device's screen through the agent and use its mouse and keyboard.

Enter one group per line, as a SID (recommended) or a name. To allow nobody, enter S-1-0-0.

Not configured: the groups of "Default groups for every right", else the groups chosen when the agent was installed (Administrators by default).

Registry: Rights\Desktop (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_DesktopViewOnly">Who may only see the screen</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_DesktopViewOnly_Help">Groups that may see the device's screen through the agent without controlling it.

Enter one group per line, as a SID (recommended) or a name.

Not configured: nobody.

Registry: Rights\DesktopViewOnly (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_SecureDesktop">Who may use the sign-in screen, lock screen and UAC prompts</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_SecureDesktop_Help">Groups that may see and use the secure desktop through the agent: the sign-in screen, the lock screen and UAC prompts.

Enter one group per line, as a SID (recommended) or a name. To allow nobody, enter S-1-0-0.

Not configured: the groups of "Default groups for every right", else the groups chosen when the agent was installed (Administrators by default).

Registry: Rights\SecureDesktop (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_ConsentBypass">Who may connect without asking the signed-in user</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_ConsentBypass_Help">Groups that may connect to a signed-in user's screen without asking them, when "Allowed ways to get the user's consent" includes "No prompt". The banner on the device still shows who is connected.

Enter one group per line, as a SID (recommended) or a name.

Not configured: nobody.

Registry: Rights\ConsentBypass (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_PrivacyScreen">Who may turn on the privacy screen</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_PrivacyScreen_Help">Groups that may black out the device's screen while they work on it. The policy "Privacy screen" must allow it too.

Enter one group per line, as a SID (recommended) or a name. To allow nobody, enter S-1-0-0.

Not configured: the groups of "Default groups for every right", else the groups chosen when the agent was installed (Administrators by default).

Registry: Rights\PrivacyScreen (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_Tunnel">Who may use this device as a jump host</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_Tunnel_Help">Groups that may connect through this device to other devices. "Allow this device as a jump host" must be on too.

Enter one group per line, as a SID (recommended) or a name. To allow nobody, enter S-1-0-0.

Not configured: the groups of "Default groups for every right", else the groups chosen when the agent was installed (Administrators by default).

Registry: Rights\Tunnel (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_AutoSignIn">Who may sign in automatically</string>
      <string id="Agent_Right_AutoSignIn_Help">Groups whose members may be signed in automatically on this device's console when nobody is signed in. "When nobody is signed in" must allow it, the credential provider must be installed, and the account also needs "Who may see and control the screen" and "Who may use the sign-in screen, lock screen and UAC prompts".

Enter one group per line, as a SID (recommended) or a name. To allow nobody, enter S-1-0-0.

Not configured: the groups of "Default groups for every right", else the groups chosen when the agent was installed (Administrators by default).

Registry: Rights\AutoSignIn (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>

      <!-- Agent: remote desktop -->
      <string id="Agent_DesktopEnabled">Allow remote desktop</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopEnabled_Help">Enabled or Not configured: the agent offers this device's screen to the accounts in "Who may see and control the screen" and "Who may only see the screen".

Disabled: nobody can connect to this device's screen through the agent. PowerShell and the other tools aren't affected.

Registry: DesktopEnabled (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopLimits">Highest frame rate and picture quality</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopLimits_Help">Upper limits for what RemoteHub may choose: frames per second (1–60) and picture quality (1–100).

Not configured: 60 frames per second and quality 100.

Registry: MaxFramesPerSecond and MaxQuality (REG_DWORD).</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopClipboard">Clipboard</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopClipboard_Help">What may be copied between RemoteHub and this device during a remote desktop. View-only connections get no clipboard.

Not configured: text, images and files.

Registry: DesktopClipboard (REG_DWORD) = 0 off, 1 text, 2 text and images, 3 text, images and files.</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopClipboard_0">Off</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopClipboard_1">Text</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopClipboard_2">Text and images</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopClipboard_3">Text, images and files</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopIdleTimeoutMinutes">End idle remote desktops</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopIdleTimeoutMinutes_Help">A remote desktop without mouse or keyboard input for this many minutes ends, after a warning shortly before. View-only connections count too. 0 = never (0–1440).

Not configured: never.

Registry: DesktopIdleTimeoutMinutes (REG_DWORD).</string>

      <string id="Agent_VirtualDisplay">Virtual display for devices without a monitor</string>
      <string id="Agent_VirtualDisplay_Help">When the agent switches on its virtual monitor during a remote desktop:
- Only when the device has no usable resolution (no monitor connected).
- Always.
- Never.

The policy can't install the driver: it comes with the agent's install option "Virtual display for servers without a monitor". Without it, the first two do nothing.

Not configured: what the install chose (only when needed, by default).

Registry: VirtualDisplay (REG_SZ) = auto, always or off.</string>
      <string id="Agent_VirtualDisplay_auto">Only when the device has no usable resolution</string>
      <string id="Agent_VirtualDisplay_always">Always</string>
      <string id="Agent_VirtualDisplay_off">Never</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopApps">Allow app tabs</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopApps_Help">Enabled or Not configured: an admin with "Who may see and control the screen" can open single apps from this device in tabs of their own in RemoteHub. Each app runs in the admin's own session on this device, on a virtual monitor of its own; the agent signs the admin in automatically when nobody is signed in. RemoteHub can also list the apps in this device's Start menu.

Disabled: no app tabs and no Start menu list. The remote desktop itself isn't affected.

App tabs need the agent's virtual display (install option "Virtual display for servers without a monitor").

Registry: DesktopApps (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopAppsElevated">Allow apps as administrator in app tabs</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopAppsElevated_Help">Enabled or Not configured: an app marked "Run as administrator" starts with the admin's full administrator rights, without a UAC prompt – the admin is an administrator on this device and signed in to the agent with their password. Every start is audited.

Disabled: such apps are refused; apps that ask for administrator rights later show the UAC prompt on the device's secure desktop.

Registry: DesktopAppsElevated (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopAppsAllowed">Programs app tabs may start</string>
      <string id="Agent_DesktopAppsAllowed_Help">The only programs that may be started in app tabs, one full path per line, for example C:\Windows\System32\mmc.exe. Environment variables such as %SystemRoot% may be used.

Not configured: any program the admin could start in their own session.

Enabled with an empty list: none.

Registry: DesktopAppsAllowed (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_MaxAppTabs">Most app tabs at once</string>
      <string id="Agent_MaxAppTabs_Help">How many app tabs may be open on this device at the same time (1–7). Each one has a virtual monitor of its own.

Not configured: 7.

Registry: MaxAppTabs (REG_DWORD).</string>

      <!-- Agent: consent -->
      <string id="Agent_AllowedConsentModes">Allowed ways to get the user's consent</string>
      <string id="Agent_AllowedConsentModes_Help">How the signed-in user agrees before someone connects to their screen:
- Ask: the user is asked and clicks Allow.
- PIN: the device shows a code the user reads out.
- No prompt: no question; needs "Who may connect without asking the signed-in user". The banner still shows.

Not configured: Ask or PIN.

Registry: AllowedConsentModes (REG_SZ, separated by ;) – Ask, Pin, None.</string>
      <string id="Agent_Consent_AskPin">Ask or PIN</string>
      <string id="Agent_Consent_Ask">Ask only</string>
      <string id="Agent_Consent_Pin">PIN only</string>
      <string id="Agent_Consent_AskPinNone">Ask, PIN or no prompt</string>
      <string id="Agent_Consent_AskNone">Ask or no prompt</string>
      <string id="Agent_Consent_PinNone">PIN or no prompt</string>
      <string id="Agent_Consent_None">No prompt only</string>
      <string id="Agent_ConsentTimeoutSeconds">Time to answer</string>
      <string id="Agent_ConsentTimeoutSeconds_Help">How many seconds the signed-in user has to answer before it counts as "Deny" (5–600).

Not configured: 30 seconds.

Registry: ConsentTimeoutSeconds (REG_DWORD).</string>

      <!-- Agent: privacy -->
      <string id="Agent_PrivacyScreen">Privacy screen</string>
      <string id="Agent_PrivacyScreen_Help">Whether an admin may black out this device's screen while working on it ("Who may turn on the privacy screen" decides who):
- Allowed, and the device's mouse and keyboard can be blocked (the admin can still leave them usable).
- Allowed, but the device's mouse and keyboard stay usable.
- Not allowed.

Not configured: allowed, and the mouse and keyboard can be blocked.

Registry: PrivacyScreen (REG_DWORD) = 2, 1 or 0.</string>
      <string id="Agent_PrivacyScreen_2">Allowed, the device's mouse and keyboard can be blocked</string>
      <string id="Agent_PrivacyScreen_1">Allowed, the device's mouse and keyboard stay usable</string>
      <string id="Agent_PrivacyScreen_0">Not allowed</string>
      <string id="Agent_PrivacyScreenMessage">Privacy screen heading</string>
      <string id="Agent_PrivacyScreenMessage_Help">The heading the person at the device sees on the privacy screen (with message), at most 120 characters, for example "Contoso IT is working on this PC". The rest of the screen stays as it is.

Not configured: "Remote Support Session in Progress".

Registry: PrivacyScreenMessage (REG_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_HiddenApps">Apps never shown to an admin</string>
      <string id="Agent_HiddenApps_Help">Apps whose windows are always covered in the admin's picture, whatever the user at the device chooses, for example a password manager.

One per line: the full path of a program (C:\Program Files\KeePass\KeePass.exe), or aumid: followed by a Store app's AppUserModelID. A Store app can also be listed by the path of its program (as Task Manager shows it, under C:\Program Files\WindowsApps); it still matches after the app updates, although its folder name carries the version. C:\Windows\System32\notepad.exe on Windows 11 only starts the Store Notepad and hides nothing – list the Store app instead.

Not configured: none; the user can still hide apps.

Registry: HiddenApps (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>

      <!-- Agent: automatic sign-in -->
      <string id="Agent_NoUserBehavior">When nobody is signed in</string>
      <string id="Agent_NoUserBehavior_Help">What a remote desktop shows on this device's console when nobody is signed in:
- Show the sign-in screen: the admin signs in on the sign-in screen.
- Allow automatic sign-in: RemoteHub may sign in with the connection's account. It also needs the credential provider (installed with the agent unless AUTOSIGNIN=0), "Who may sign in automatically", and RemoteHub must not have it turned off (RemoteHub Console → Turn off automatic sign-in through the agent).

Not configured: automatic sign-in is allowed, unless the agent was installed with it turned off (AUTOSIGNIN=0, or Remove credential provider in RemoteHub). To turn it off on every device, enable this policy with "Show the sign-in screen".

Registry: NoUserBehavior (REG_SZ) = SignInScreen or AutoSignIn.</string>
      <string id="Agent_NoUserBehavior_SignInScreen">Show the sign-in screen</string>
      <string id="Agent_NoUserBehavior_AutoSignIn">Allow automatic sign-in</string>
      <string id="Agent_AutoSignInRequirePrivacyScreen">Only with the privacy screen</string>
      <string id="Agent_AutoSignInRequirePrivacyScreen_Help">Enabled: no automatic sign-in where the privacy screen can't be shown (Windows before 10 2004 / Server 2022, or "Privacy screen" not allowed).

Disabled or Not configured: automatic sign-in works there too.

Registry: AutoSignInRequirePrivacyScreen (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>
      <string id="Agent_AutoSignInEndAction">When the connection ends</string>
      <string id="Agent_AutoSignInEndAction_Help">What happens to a session the agent signed in automatically when its connection ends: lock it, or sign it out (unsaved work is lost). This wins over the connection's setting in RemoteHub.

Not configured: RemoteHub's setting decides (Lock by default).

Registry: AutoSignInEndAction (REG_SZ) = Lock or SignOut.</string>
      <string id="Agent_AutoSignInEndAction_Lock">Lock the session</string>
      <string id="Agent_AutoSignInEndAction_SignOut">Sign out</string>

      <!-- Agent: jump host -->
      <string id="Agent_TunnelEnabled">Allow this device as a jump host</string>
      <string id="Agent_TunnelEnabled_Help">Enabled or Not configured: RemoteHub can reach other devices through this device's agent ("Who may use this device as a jump host" decides who).

Disabled: this device is never a jump host.

Registry: TunnelEnabled (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>
      <string id="Agent_TunnelTargets">Where jump connections may go</string>
      <string id="Agent_TunnelTargets_Help">The addresses and names connections through this device may go to. One per line: address ranges (10.20.0.0/16, fd00::/8, 10.1.2.3) and names (sql01.contoso.local, *.contoso.local).

This device's own addresses are only allowed when an entry names that exact address. Loopback, link-local (169.254.0.0/16, for example cloud metadata) and multicast addresses are only allowed by a range of /8 or narrower that contains them.

Not configured: anywhere, except those special addresses.

Registry: TunnelTargets (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_TunnelPorts">Ports jump connections may go to</string>
      <string id="Agent_TunnelPorts_Help">The ports connections through this device may go to. One per line: a port (3389), a range (5985-5986) or * for all.

Not configured: every port.

Registry: TunnelPorts (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_MaxTunnelStreams">Connections per jump connection</string>
      <string id="Agent_MaxTunnelStreams_Help">How many connections may be open at once through one jump connection (1–1024).

Not configured: 64.

Registry: MaxTunnelStreams (REG_DWORD).</string>

      <string id="Agent_TunnelOwnPorts">Ports on this device itself</string>
      <string id="Agent_TunnelOwnPorts_Help">The ports on this device itself that connections through its agent may reach – RDP through the agent, for example when the device is reached only through the RemoteHub Broker. One per line: RDP (the port RDP is configured for on this device) or a port number such as 1433. Services that listen only on this device stay unreachable unless they are listed.

Not configured: RDP.

Enabled with an empty list: none.

Registry: TunnelOwnPorts (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>

      <!-- Agent: RemoteHub Broker -->
      <string id="Agent_BrokerEnabled">Allow the RemoteHub Broker</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerEnabled_Help">Enabled or Not configured: when the agent was installed or joined with a RemoteHub Broker, it keeps an outbound connection to it, so RemoteHub can reach this device from anywhere. The device's certificate pin and the Windows sign-in still apply to every session.

Disabled: the agent never connects to a RemoteHub Broker; the broker in its configuration is ignored.

Registry: BrokerEnabled (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerUrl">Broker address</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerUrl_Help">Another address for the same RemoteHub Broker and organisation, for example after the broker moved to a new name: https://rh.contoso.com.

Not configured: the address the agent was installed or joined with.

Registry: BrokerUrl (REG_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerConsoles">Consoles allowed through the broker</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerConsoles_Help">The RemoteHub consoles (their console IDs, as the broker lists them) that may start a session with this device through the RemoteHub Broker. One per line.

Not configured: every console registered with the organisation.

Registry: BrokerConsoles (REG_MULTI_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerHttpProxy">HTTP proxy for the broker</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerHttpProxy_Help">The HTTP proxy the agent uses for its connections to the RemoteHub Broker, for example http://proxy.contoso.com:8080.

Not configured: the system's proxy setting.

Registry: BrokerHttpProxy (REG_SZ).</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerKeepAliveSeconds">Keep-alive interval (seconds)</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerKeepAliveSeconds_Help">How often the agent sends a keep-alive on its connection to the RemoteHub Broker (30–90 seconds). Shorter keeps the connection through firewalls and proxies that close quiet connections early, and shows a device that lost power as offline sooner (after about 2.5 intervals); longer sends less (about 220 bytes each time).

Not configured: 60.

Registry: BrokerKeepAliveSeconds (REG_DWORD).</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerDirect">Direct connections through the broker</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerDirect_Help">Whether a session through the RemoteHub Broker may also run directly between RemoteHub and this device – UDP with hole punching, then QUIC, both ends pinned – instead of through the broker's relay:
- Over public and local addresses: also over the device's own adapter addresses, so a console in the same network or VPN connects directly.
- Over public addresses only: only over the public address a STUN service sees; the device's own addresses aren't sent to the broker.
- Off – relayed only: no UDP and no candidates; every session runs through the relay (TCP 443).

Not configured: over public and local addresses. Devices before Windows 11 / Server 2022 are always relayed.

Registry: BrokerDirect (REG_DWORD) = 2, 1 or 0.</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerDirect_2">Over public and local addresses</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerDirect_1">Over public addresses only</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerDirect_0">Off – relayed only</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerStun">STUN service for direct connections</string>
      <string id="Agent_BrokerStun_Help">The STUN service the agent asks for its public address on the direct path, as name:port, for example stun.contoso.com:3478. It needs outbound UDP to it.

Not configured: the one the RemoteHub Broker names (by default stun.cloudflare.com:3478).

Registry: BrokerStun (REG_SZ).</string>

      <!-- Agent: service -->
      <string id="Agent_AuditToEventLog">Audit to the Windows Application log</string>
      <string id="Agent_AuditToEventLog_Help">Enabled or Not configured: the agent's audit entries (sign-ins, sessions, remote desktops, jump connections, automatic sign-ins) also go to the Windows Application log, source "RemoteHub Agent", for event forwarding or a SIEM.

Disabled: they are only in the agent's log files.

Registry: AuditToEventLog (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>

      <string id="Agent_ListenPort">Port</string>
      <string id="Agent_ListenPort_Help">The TCP port the agent listens on (1–65535). Read when the service starts: restart the RemoteHub Agent service (or the device) after changing it. The agent moves its firewall rule to the new port.

RemoteHub connects to the port stored for each device. After changing it, set the same port in RemoteHub's connections (or install the agent again from RemoteHub), or RemoteHub can't reach the device.

Not configured: the port chosen when the agent was installed (58732 by default).

Registry: ListenPort (REG_DWORD).</string>
      <string id="Agent_ListenEnabled">Listen on the network</string>
      <string id="Agent_ListenEnabled_Help">Enabled or Not configured: the agent listens on its port (ListenPort), and RemoteHub can reach it over the network – directly, or through a jump host.

Disabled: the agent opens no port and switches its firewall rule off. The device is then reachable only through a RemoteHub Broker, which the agent connects to outbound. Without a broker set up, nothing can reach the device from the network – only someone at the device itself can change that.

Read when the service starts: restart the RemoteHub Agent service (or the device) after changing it. Enabled again, the firewall rule is switched back on with the scope it was installed with; a device installed with BROKER_ONLY=1 has no rule and keeps having none.

Registry: ListenEnabled (REG_DWORD) = 1 or 0.</string>
      <string id="Agent_LogRetentionDays">Keep log files</string>
      <string id="Agent_LogRetentionDays_Help">How many days the agent keeps its daily log files in %ProgramData%\RemoteHub\Agent\logs (1–3650). Older files are removed once a day.

The Windows Application log ("Audit to the Windows Application log") has its own size and retention.

Not configured: 30 days, or what agent.json says.

Registry: LogRetentionDays (REG_DWORD).</string>
      <string id="Agent_MaxSessions">Sessions at once</string>
      <string id="Agent_MaxSessions_Help">How many PowerShell, remote desktop and jump connections the agent accepts at the same time (1–200). Further connections are refused until one ends.

Not configured: 20, or what agent.json says.

Registry: MaxSessions (REG_DWORD).</string>
    </stringTable>

